00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.070 align:start position:0%
so we've heard so much about leveling up  over the last year or so haven't we not  just from those who propose it but also  from those who protest they don't really  know what it means and historically  there's been a lot written and a lot  said about the inequalities between  north and south but at this very moment  that we're at now post pandemic what are  the challenges and the opportunities as  well to getting this country well and  truly leveled so first of all where are  we at i want to address head on that  notion that the north routinely gets  less spent on it than the south is that  true so take a look at this firstly i  want you to pay attention only to how  high up the blobs are on this graph  first  what we've  charted along this axis here is the  total spend  per head in each region of the country  by the government in 2020 so total look  london up there at the top uh is the  most but look the northeast and the  north west actually come in second and  third however total spend doesn't really  give us the whole picture because total  spend includes things like nhs salaries  and benefits and money that sort of is  there and then it's gone so what we've  plotted along at the access at the  bottom is total investment spend  for each region of the country  investment spend going into  infrastructure and things like that and  as you can see a slightly different  story again london out there at the the  far end southeast coming second but and  although the north west not so bad the  northeast yorkshire in the humber much  further over towards here so a very  different story now crucially this is  just a moment in time this is just 2020  and of course what happens over time  incredibly important as well so what  we've charted here is the growth in  investment spend since 2016. 2016 of  course you remember just shortly after  we very first heard about the northern  powerhouse actually you can see up in  the top there the green line is the west  midlands that has had its spend grow the  most in terms of investment that's  likely a lot to do with hs2 but the  southeast coming in second there and  crucially i just want to draw your  attention to the yellow line at the  bottom yorkshire and the humber where we  currently are really one of the only  regions in the country where spend  really declined over that period peaking  just about to what it was in 2016 very  very recently there now look in terms of  regional recovery and regional  prosperity it's not just what the  government is doing that matters but  it's also what we as consumers are doing  and how we're behaving so what we've  charted here is footfall  footfall recovery in cities around the  country since the pandemic compared to  pre-pandemic levels for obvious reasons  we haven't been able to put every single  one on there could be a lot but i've  just picked out a few that i think tell  interesting stories firstly the two at  the top blackpool south end it's worth  saying there are only four cities in  four cities and towns in the whole  country where footfall is now recovered  to what it was before the pandemic or  indeed exceeding it they're all seaside  and touristy towns down at the bottom  there london sheffield that big story of  city centres just really struggling to  recover in terms of their footfall and  then in the orange barnsley we've put  that on there because there's a really  interesting story around some of these  sort of smaller regional residential  towns actually some that historically  have been a little more deprived some of  the footfall and the spenders recovered  much much better there than in other  places that's partly uh because of it's  more it's more residential people live  there and also because a lot of the  businesses in those places are more  likely to be essential so people need to  get back there for their essential spend  it matters as well what business is  doing of course a number of companies  the number of jobs the amount of  employment what we've shown you here is  the growth in the number of companies in  each region of the country since 2010 so  since that financial crisis and look  it's really clear isn't it london the  south east the south west it is still  those regions that have by far the  biggest growth in the number of  companies and we know that there's  well-documented problems with qualified  people sometimes choosing to leave uh to  travel south particularly to the capital  for work and very finally i want to  leave you with this this is incidentally  my favorite chart of the day and i think  it gives a slightly quirky look into  some of the problems we face this shows  the inequality of broadband connectivity  in different countries across the world  so the red blobs the least connected  region in each country the green blobs  the most connected region in each  country essentially what this is showing  us is that we have a greater inequality  of connectivity in this country than  colombia has certainly a greater  inequality of connectivity than denmark  has and look our worst connected region  the northeast there is by far worse off  than the vast majority of all these  other countries the u.s france spain  worth saying the same picture plays out  when you look at germany italy portugal  so i hope this gives a bit of a sense of  just how much there is to do and just  how multi-faceted the problems are if we  really want to get this country well and  truly leveled up
 

